{"kind":"report","text":"ready-for-review at exact clean head e1c7886db74f1700ef646fd939a719463fb98e59, artifact bcb505958954d9ecedd57f6b58a435b4e5522bf2. Two conditions are partly unmet and I name them at the end rather than let them pass.\n\nI was wrong about the premise, and the correction improves the work. I twice told Hugh the UI does not display sequence numbers, having checked `shortEvent` in ui/src/lib/api.ts and found an eight-character hash truncation. It does display them: `ticketsOf` in ui/src/lib/store.ts maps each decision to its index plus one and SequencePane renders `#N`. Hugh was right and I checked the wrong file, twice, while stating the correction confidently.\n\nWhat that changes is the shape of the fix rather than the need for it. The number existed only in the UI, derived from where a decision landed in an array — a fact about one response rather than about the log, which would have quietly disagreed with every other reader the moment a projection arrived filtered or paged. It is now derived in the fold from the per-record index, and the UI reads it, falling back to the old rule so an older resident still renders. The two agree today by coincidence of ordering, which is exactly why the coincidence should not have been load-bearing.\n\nThe number is stable because it is derived rather than assigned, and a test pins that across a re-fold of the same log, together with two properties worth stating: nothing is #0, since the founding seed is #1, and no two statements share a number. Statement sequences are not 1..n — they skip ratify and supersede records — and the test says so, because a reader who expected contiguity would misread a gap as a missing event.\n\nProfileVersion advances to workroom-fold@2. Nothing semantic moved, so the letter of \"any semantic fold change must advance this\" does not obviously apply, but a resident reusing a @1 checkpoint would serve #0 for every event. Silently wrong numbers are worse than absent ones, and this is a number people are now told to say out loud. The two pinned projections are regenerated for the same reason: that guard exists to force an explicit migration decision, and this is that decision rather than a fixture refresh.\n\nSKILL.md separates the two names instead of banning one. Cite with the canonical identifier, which is the only name the fold resolves and must be copied whole from the result that returned it; say `#N` in prose, where a number can be checked by eye and a 40-hex string cannot. It also says `#N` means nothing outside its workroom, so anything crossing that boundary keeps the canonical form. The fold's citation resolution is untouched, as the conditions require — making `#N` resolvable would replace one silent failure with a subtler one.\n\nNot done, and I would rather report it than stretch the conditions. The CLI's human-readable output has not been swept: `gs status` still prints what it printed. And `shortEvent`/`shortHash` in the UI still exist and are still called from Composer.tsx and Railway.tsx, so the truncation that manufactured this habit is reduced but not removed. Those two are the remainder of the same condition and should be finished before this closes; I stopped because the fold, the wire and the contract are a coherent reviewable unit and the sweep is a second one.\n\nGates: `go build ./...`, `go vet ./...`, the full `go test ./internal/... ./cmd/...` suite, `go test ./internal/docset -count=1`, `npx tsc --noEmit` and `npm test` (69 passing) all pass at this head. The worktree is clean.","body":{"head":"e1c7886db74f1700ef646fd939a719463fb98e59","status":"ready-for-review"}}